  [1mShort design[0m

  [1mApproach — word boundary, with a hard-cut fallback.[0m Cut back to the last whitespace before the budget; if the budget
  contains no whitespace (one long unbroken word or a URL), hard-cut instead. That's four lines more than the pure hard
  cut and removes its worst output — the mid-word amputation. Pure hard cut is simpler but I don't think the simplicity
  is worth it here: the only thing it buys is the absence of one [38;5;153mlastIndexOf[39m call, and it's the choice you'd revisit the
  first time a label reads [38;5;153m"Configu..."[39m. A third option — a [38;5;153mtruncate: 'word' | 'exact'[39m strategy setting — I'm
  discarding as YAGNI; two behaviors in a utility with no callers yet is speculative.

  [1mBehavior:[0m
  - [38;5;153moptions.maxLength[39m is a number. Strings at or under it come back untouched.
  - Budget is [38;5;153mmaxLength - 3[39m; slice there, cut back to the last whitespace if there is one, trim trailing whitespace,
    append [38;5;153m'...'[39m. Result is never longer than [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m.
  - [38;5;153mmaxLength <= 3[39m leaves no room for content: return [38;5;153m'...'.slice(0, maxLength)[39m. Keeps the never-exceed guarantee
    instead of throwing.

  [1mOne ordering decision worth flagging:[0m the truncate block goes [1mlast[0m, after [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m. If it ran earlier, a prefix
  would push the result back over [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m and the guarantee you just picked would be false. The cost is that [38;5;153mmaxLength
[39m  can eat into a suffix — with a short budget, [38;5;153m{ suffix: " (new)", maxLength: 10 }[39m can truncate away part of the
  suffix. I think a real maximum is worth that; say so if you'd rather the suffix be protected.

